The '''Abyalan Cold War''' ({{langx|bl|Guerra Fria Abyalana}}; {{Langx|ca|Guerra Freda Abyalan}}) was a political rivalry in the [[Western Hemisphere]] from the early 1920s to the late 1980s and a part of the wider [[Red Scare]]. It is generally accepted that the beginning of the Abyalan Cold War is marked by the [[Baliscan Revolution]] of 1918-20, which led to the formation of the first socialist state outside of Fosia. Thereafter, newly formed socialist movements across Abyala, inspired by these successes, would engage in militant political and armed struggle against existing state regimes, often with [[Baliscan military internationalism|Baliscan support]].

The Abyalan Cold War was linked to the global wave of fear and suppression targeting left-wing movements, termed the Red Scare, that followed "fall" of [[Darasian Socialist Revolution|Daras]] and Balisca, two of the world's most powerful countries, to socialist revolutions in the early 20th century. The Abyalan revolutionary movements espoused [[Peripherism|peripherist]], [[W:Proletarian internationalism|internationalist]], [[W:Anti-imperialism|anti-imperialist]], and [[W:Anti-colonial|anti-colonial]] sentiment, while in contrast, the anti-revolutionary movement supported conservative right-wing (often times [[Fascism in Abyala|fascist]]) governments, such as the [[Military dictatorship in Potocsí|right-wing dictatorship in Potocsí]].

The beginning of the [[Internal conflict in Potocsí]] and the [[Adonian Community Mission for Lacashe|international intervention in Lacashe]] are widely seen as the end of this period of [[W:Great power competition|great power competition]].

